# minimum required Qore module
%requires qore >= 2.3

%modern

%requires(reexport) Util
%requires Logger
%requires(reexport) HttpServerUtil

module AsyncSocketIo {
    version = "1.0";
    desc    = "API for async socket I/O";
    author  = "David Nichols <david@qore.org>";
    url     = "http://qore.org";
    license = "MIT";
    init = sub () {
        # Pre-initialize the global controller singleton (uses AsyncIoController::get() internally)
        AsyncSocketIo::globalController = new AsyncSocketIo::AsyncSocketIoController(True);
    };
    del = sub () {
        # Just delete the Qore wrapper; the C++ singleton is stopped by
        # qore_async_io_controller_cleanup() in qore_cleanup() after all
        # modules have been unloaded
        if (AsyncSocketIo::globalController) {
            delete AsyncSocketIo::globalController;
        }
    };
}

/** @mainpage AsyncSocketIo Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section asyncsocketiointro AsyncSocketIo Module Introduction

    The %AsyncSocketIo module provides an API for async socket I/O.

    To use this module, use \c "%requires AsyncSocketIo" in your code.

    All the public symbols in the module are defined in the AsyncSocketIo namespace.

    @section asyncsocketiorelnotes Release Notes

    @subsection asyncsocketio_1_0 AsyncSocketIo 1.0
    - the initial version of the AsyncSocketIo module
    - thin wrapper around the native C++ @ref Qore::AsyncIoController class for high-performance
      async socket I/O using epoll (Linux) / kqueue (macOS/BSD)
    - @ref AsyncSocketIo::AsyncSocketIoController "AsyncSocketIoController" with submit(), exec(),
      cancel(), cancelByKey(), cancelByOwner(), wake() operations
    - custom key support in \c SocketPollOperationInfo
    - @ref AsyncSocketIo::Http2StreamContext "Http2StreamContext" stream-scoped send/receive helper
      for HTTP/2 sockets with direct I/O mode for handler-thread-driven frame reading
    - @ref AsyncSocketIo::Http3StreamContext "Http3StreamContext" stream-scoped send/receive helper
      for HTTP/3 QUIC streams with backpressure handling and I/O controller wake integration
    - @ref AsyncSocketIo::AbstractStreamContext "AbstractStreamContext" abstract base class providing a
      protocol-agnostic stream I/O interface for WebSocket over HTTP/2 (RFC 8441) and HTTP/3 (RFC 9220)
    - \c getThreadPool() for lazy
      access to a shared @ref Qore::Thread::ThreadPool "ThreadPool" for dispatching callbacks off
      the I/O thread
    - @ref AsyncSocketIo::AsyncSocketIoController::submitTask() "submitTask()" convenience
      method for submitting work to the thread pool
    - global singleton via @ref AsyncSocketIo::getGlobalAsyncIoController() "getGlobalAsyncIoController()"

    @section asyncsocketiointernals Internal Design

    This module provides a thin wrapper around the native C++ @ref Qore::AsyncIoController class,
    which implements a high-performance event loop for managing multiple concurrent socket operations.
    The C++ controller uses the most efficient I/O mechanism available on each platform (epoll on Linux,
    kqueue on macOS/BSD) via the @ref Qore::EventLoop class.

    The controller runs a dedicated I/O thread that processes socket events using a three-phase polling cycle:
    - Phase 1 (Snapshot under lock): collect operations needing continuePoll()
    - Phase 2 (continuePoll outside lock): drive operations, collect results
    - Phase 3 (Update under lock + deferred delivery): update cache/EventLoop, deliver callbacks

    This module adds backward-compatible wrapper methods and the global controller singleton on top
    of the C++ implementation.
*/

#! The AsyncSocketIo namespace
/** All public declarations in the %AsyncSocketIo module are defined in this namespace
*/
public namespace AsyncSocketIo {
}
